---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc';
const title = 'Review requests and UGC permission for Shopify SMS | ' + site.name;
const desc = 'Plan neutral review requests around delivered items, customer identity, permission, exits, duplicate events, and separate UGC reuse approval.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Reviews and UGC</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Ask about the product experience, then ask separately about reuse</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A delivered parcel creates a possible review moment. It does not establish who used every item, permission to text a gift recipient, or permission to turn a photo into an advertisement. Build those decisions into the request queue before choosing an SMS app.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app options</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post-purchase messages</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define the review episode</h2>
      <p>Keep a stable episode record: store, order, line item, variant, customer, intended recipient, delivery evidence, request purpose, and policy version. Record the payer separately from the person invited to describe actual use. A customer profile or shared phone number is not enough to merge two people's experiences. Avoid placing personal details or order access credentials in public review links.</p>
      <p>Use item-level delivery evidence where available. An order with one delivered item and one backordered item should not produce a request saying both have arrived. If only parcel-level information exists and its contents cannot be resolved, hold the episode for reconciliation. Carrier delivery is an input, not proof that the customer has opened, assembled, worn, or used the product.</p>
      <p>Choose a use-context delay by product and observed fulfillment conditions. A print can be inspected differently from furniture awaiting assembly. Document the assumption and a latest useful request time; do not apply one universal day-after-purchase schedule. A delayed delivery should replace the earlier due date rather than add a second reminder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Identity and permission come before timing</h2>
      <p>Check evidence of permission for the intended message category, channel, number, and store immediately before dispatch. A shipping phone field or purchase alone is not an operational substitute for that evidence. If identity, permission scope, or destination timezone is unresolved, hold or suppress the text. Apply the destination rules and sender restrictions relevant to your program; a global send window is not a compliance guarantee.</p>
      <p>For a gift order, the payer may be able to review ordering or delivery but may never use the gift. Do not silently retarget the gift recipient from shipping details. Invite a product review only through a separately established identity and permitted channel. Keep service feedback and product-use feedback distinct so neither is represented as the other.</p>
      <p>Check the combined email and SMS calendar, opt-outs, channel suppression, and the review platform's own request automation. Name one owner for each episode. Read <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent operations</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">destination timing</a>, and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email–SMS coordination</a> when designing these gates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Solicit honest feedback without screening for praise</h2>
      <p>Use neutral copy: “If you have had a chance to use your mug, would you share your experience? [review link]”. This is an illustrative draft for an otherwise eligible recipient; add the sender identification and messaging controls required for the actual program. Do not ask only customers who clicked a happy-face survey, purchased repeatedly, or gave support a high score. Provide the same review opportunity regardless of expected sentiment.</p>
      <p>Keep support available without making it a detour that blocks critical reviews. An open return or support case can pause automated requests to avoid pressure during resolution, using the same rule regardless of sentiment. Do not erase submitted criticism or permanently remove dissatisfied customers from the review opportunity. After resolution, reassess eligibility under a documented policy rather than automatically replaying the missed send.</p>
      <p>The US <a class="link" href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/consumer-reviews-testimonials-rule-questions-answers">FTC review-rule Q&amp;A</a> explains that incentives cannot be conditioned expressly or implicitly on a particular review sentiment. That is a specific US rule constraint, not blanket approval of an incentive program. Before any reward, verify the receiving platform's current terms and applicable disclosure requirements. Start without incentives if those checks are unresolved; never promise a reward for five stars, changing criticism, or removing a truthful negative review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>A review submission is separate from UGC reuse</h2>
      <p>Track review submitted, photo uploaded, reuse requested, reuse approved, and reuse declined as distinct states. Do not infer advertising permission from a review, an image attachment, a social tag, or an SMS opt-in. Use a separate approval process that identifies the asset and intended placements, editing, attribution, duration, and any compensation. Have the rights owner confirm the proposed use; escalate uncertain ownership or other people appearing in an image before publication.</p>
      <p>Keep the approval text, version, asset identifier, identity evidence, and decision together. A grant for a product-page gallery should not silently authorize a paid social campaign. Route a changed placement or edit beyond the recorded scope back for review. Maintain a removal and withdrawal request queue with an owner and an inventory of placements to assess; do not promise universal immediate removal from every downstream copy.</p>
      <p>A reuse decline closes the reuse request without changing the customer's review or service access. A published asset needs a separate placement record and any required disclosure review. None of these workflow states by itself establishes that all rights or advertising obligations have been satisfied.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Queue transitions, exits, and recovery</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Event or state</th><th>Review-request action</th><th>Recovery rule</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Delivered with known item and identity</td><td>Schedule after a plausible use interval</td><td>Replace stale delivery estimates; retain one episode</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Review submitted</td><td>Close request and all reminders for that episode</td><td>A late delivery event cannot reopen it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Return, refund, cancellation, or open support</td><td>Cancel or pause unsent jobs using the documented event policy</td><td>Resolution requires fresh eligibility; no backlog burst</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opt-out or permission mismatch</td><td>Suppress before the sender call</td><td>No retry on another number or channel without its own permission</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unknown sender outcome</td><td>Reconcile message status before retrying</td><td>Use provider idempotency where supported; never assume timeout means unsent</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reuse approval declined or unclear</td><td>Close or hold the reuse job</td><td>Do not publish; keep review status independent</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Deduplicate event IDs and use a stable episode-and-purpose key for sends. Keep event time, received time, and source version: an old delivery webhook must not override a later return or submission. At dispatch, reread authoritative review, fulfillment, permission, and support state. A stale export must not win over a live suppression.</p>
      <p>Keep failed event joins, missing recipient identities, unresolved delivery contents, and sender failures in separate queues. Assign an owner, expiry, reason, and reconciliation log. Recover in bounded batches after checking current state and campaign capacity. Expired requests should close rather than send weeks later. A new purchase can create a new episode without reviving a closed request for the previous item.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are proposed acceptance fixtures for a store's implementation, not merchant results or claims about any vendor.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Split order:</strong> a mug is delivered while a blanket remains unshipped. Expect only the mug episode to become eligible after its use interval; no blanket review link.</li>
        <li><strong>Gift purchase:</strong> the payer has SMS permission but the shipping recipient does not. Expect no recipient text and no claim that the payer used the product.</li>
        <li><strong>Submitted before reminder:</strong> a review arrives while a reminder waits. Expect the reminder to close, including after a duplicated delivery event.</li>
        <li><strong>Return during delay:</strong> a return opens before dispatch. Expect the unsent job to pause or close; resolving the return must not release a stale message automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Negative feedback:</strong> a customer reports disappointment. Expect the same review path as other eligible customers and access to support without a requirement to remove criticism.</li>
        <li><strong>Photo without reuse approval:</strong> a customer attaches a photo to a review. Expect no advertising placement until the separate scope and approval checks pass.</li>
        <li><strong>Ambiguous timeout:</strong> the sender accepts a request but the response is lost. Expect status reconciliation before retry, with no duplicate request after confirmation.</li>
        <li><strong>Late events and backlog:</strong> an old delivery event arrives after a refund while the worker recovers from an outage. Expect the refund state to prevail and expired jobs to close before batch release.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure submissions separately from reusable assets</h2>
      <p>Randomize eligible customers into request and holdout groups before dispatch, keeping their episodes together across channels. Define the observation window and ordinary review invitations received by both groups. Compare all assigned eligible customers, including failed sends; restricting analysis to delivered messages can hide operational failure. Keep assignment stable through retries and late events.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical worksheet, 120 of 1,000 assigned request customers submit a review, versus 100 of 1,000 holdout customers. Those are 12% and 10%, a difference of 2 percentage points, or 20 additional submitters per 1,000 assigned customers. This arithmetic is not evidence of lift; uncertainty, imbalance, and cross-channel exposure still need assessment. Count unique submitters separately from review count when one person reviews multiple items.</p>
      <p>Report invitations attempted, accepted by sender, delivered, clicked, submitted, and published as different stages. Publication may depend on moderation and is not guaranteed. Separately count reuse approvals, approved assets actually placed, and placement removals. Include opt-outs, complaints, unresolved support contacts, and queue age. Avoid treating a larger average star rating as the program's objective.</p>
      <p>For costs, include platform allocation, messages and carrier charges, integration work, rewards if used, moderation, rights review, and support. If the hypothetical incremental program cost is $200 and the estimated difference is 20 submitters, the arithmetic is $10 per additional submitter. If the difference is zero or uncertain, do not present that ratio as a stable acquisition cost. Measure sales contribution in a separate experiment rather than assigning every later order to a review request.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose tools around the state you can actually maintain</h2>
      <p>A single messaging workspace can fit when the team can synchronize review submission and suppression before every send. Separate email, SMS, review, and asset systems can fit when one episode ledger owns scheduling and reconciliation. In either architecture, require a demonstration of your own split-order, submitted-review, timeout, and permission fixtures before enabling requests.</p>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> to build a shortlist and <a class="link" href="/compare">comparisons</a> to frame questions. Verify current integration events, plan access, costs, moderation exports, suppression latency, and rights-record portability directly with each supplier. An app that sends SMS is not thereby a review host or a UGC rights manager.</p>
      <p>Start with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">measurement</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> when planning a pilot. Launch only when someone owns the exception queues and the stop rule. The useful outcome is an honest, attributable account of customer experience and separately documented reuse decisions, not a promised number of positive reviews.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
